This `nut' is Jim Bidzos, president of RSA.  He's probably bluffing,
but don't take that as legal advice.  The lawsuit would probably be
for "inducing infringement", or something of that sort.  Unless
you've really been irritating RSA in an unusual fashion (and there
are a lot of other people distributing PGP), they *prob*ab*ly* won't
go after you.  But the bottom line is that they can sue you if they
wish, and you have to defend yourself.  Whether you want to risk
such a suit would depend both on whether you care for the hassle,
and whether you think you can line up the resources to do a good job
in court.  You don't want to set a bad precedent.

It sounds somewhat form-letterish, with a preliminary introduction
message, and then a canned spiel.  But RSA could really be deciding
to try for some blood.  In any case, you might consider popularizing
this new attitude of RSA's.  Rather poor timing, I'd say.  You'll
have to decide how you want to deal with this.

